my name is Angus Trumbull and I'm senior  curator of paintings and sculpture here  at the Yale Center for British art at  first book a brief history of the smile  began when I was asked to give a talk at  a conference of dentists and after that  the dentists doctors and dentists talk  to each other so the dentist's  recommended me to the orthopedic  surgeons who were having a conference  about sports injuries and I was asked to  give a talk at that conference and so I  gave a talk about gesture and that is  what led me ultimately to write the  finger a handbook quite a big chunk of  my book is about pointing rude pointing  useful pointing pointing to indicate but  also pointing as a technique of military  command which is very ancient here in  this marvelous portrait of Admiral Lord  Bridport we see pointing used for both  military command and indication because  with the same hand the Admiral points to  his flagship the royal gorge in the  background this is an actress mrs.  abington portrayed by sir joshua  reynolds in the part of miss peru in  Congreve's love for love which was a  Restoration comedy that enjoyed a very  successful revival in the later 18th  century a lot of the book is about body  language but there's also a lot in that  about the thumb and one of the original  questions that i started with is is the  thumb or finger and i'm pleased to say  that that question was answered  resoundingly in the affirmative the  thumb is the finger that put us on top  of the evolutionary tree it's a  possibility which with each of the other  digits is the top makes the hand that  the marvelous tool that it is in this  picture though we see the thumb deployed  as a sort of fascinator the way miss  Paroo plays  the thumb over her lower lip is a  marvelously sensual device deployed by  Reynolds with very disarming subtle tea  in this representation of the sense of  touch the artist philippe Mercier  divides the composition into two halves  a pair of lovers on the right and  another pair who have been disrupted by  an angry cat that scratched the young  man's hand pain and pleasure the sense  of touch is is is an important  protagonist in in my book and it was the  sense of touch that as far back as  Aristotle gave the ancients the hardest  time to make sense of the eyes were  clearly the seat of vision the ears were  clearly the seat of hearing the nose was  clearly the siege of smell but Aristotle  had great difficulty deciding where it  was exactly that the organ which organ  was that touch related to he decided  deprived of any knowledge of the central  nervous system he decided on the heart  happenstance and serendipity played a  big part in in this project but there's  a larger principle as well because if  you cut deeply enough into one narrow  seam of human experience or in this case  Anatomy hopefully you going access to  rather more and it was my hope that by  writing a book about fingers I could  allow in the end the finger itself to  point to various interesting profound  and intriguing aspects of us
 

